  <h1>Types of Workplace Harassment</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Sexual harassment</h2><p>Unwelcome sexual advances, requests for sexual favors, or other verbal/physical conduct of a sexual nature. Two forms: quid pro quo (submission required for employment benefit) and hostile environment (severe or pervasive sexual conduct).</p>
  <h2>Racial and national origin harassment</h2><p>Racial slurs, offensive jokes, derogatory comments, or other racially hostile conduct. Among the most litigated Title VII claims. Pattern evidence (systematic demeaning treatment of employees of a particular race) is particularly compelling.</p>
  <h2>Disability-based harassment</h2><p>Mocking, demeaning, or creating a hostile environment for an employee based on physical or mental disability. Actionable under the ADA. The same severe-or-pervasive standard applies.</p>
  <h2>Age-based harassment</h2><p>Under the ADEA, a pattern of age-related derogatory comments or conduct targeting employees 40+. Isolated age jokes rarely qualify; systematic demeaning treatment can.</p>
  <h2>Religious harassment</h2><p>Hostile conduct based on an employee&rsquo;s religion or religious practice, including mocking religious dress, prayer, or beliefs. Employers also have an obligation to reasonably accommodate religious practices absent undue hardship.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-hostile-work-environment-claims-work">how these claims work</a>.</p>
